# PipeWire and WirePlumber come from trixie-backports (ADR-0002), through a
# dated snapshot rather than deb.debian.org. Backports keeps only its newest
# upload, so a live backports source makes the image depend on the day it was
# built, and the exact pin in sonora-backports.pref.chroot would stop
# resolving on the next upload. The snapshot keeps the pinned version
# available.
#
# check-valid-until=no: a snapshot's Release file expires a week after its
# date, and apt would otherwise reject it as stale.
#
# http on purpose: apt still checks the InRelease signature and every hash in
# it, and the exact pins plus the hook catch a replayed older snapshot for
# PipeWire and WirePlumber, the only packages taken from it. https would need
# ca-certificates before this source's first update, which neither the chroot
# nor tools/Dockerfile.runtime has (ADR-0002, 2026-11-02 amendment).
#
# Build-time only. live-build removes *.chroot archives after the hooks have
# run, so the shipped system carries no snapshot source.
#
# tools/Dockerfile.runtime installs this same file, so the container tests run
# against the PipeWire the image ships. Bump the timestamp together with the
# versions in the .pref file, the hook and tools/test-image-config.sh.
deb [check-valid-until=no] http://snapshot.debian.org/archive/debian/20261101T000000Z trixie-backports main
